1. Comparative Advantage and Gains from Trade
Paper: International Economics (ECNCC301) | Unit: I | Topic: Comparative advantage and trade
Introduction
Why do nations trade even when one is more efficient at producing everything? The classical answer, built by Adam Smith and perfected by David Ricardo, is the principle of comparative advantage. It is the oldest and most durable theory in international economics.
From absolute to comparative advantage
Smith argued that a country should export goods it produces with lower absolute labour cost and import the rest. This fails to explain trade when a country has no absolute advantage in anything. Ricardo (1817) showed that trade is still mutually beneficial if countries differ in relative opportunity costs. A country has a comparative advantage in the good whose opportunity cost is lower at home than abroad.
The Ricardian model
Assumptions: two countries, two goods, one factor (labour), constant returns, labour mobile within but not between countries, no transport costs, perfect competition. Example: Portugal needs 80 hours of labour for a unit of wine and 90 for a unit of cloth; England needs 120 for wine and 100 for cloth. Portugal has an absolute advantage in both goods. But Portugal's opportunity cost of cloth is 90/80 = 1.125 units of wine, while England's is 100/120 = 0.83 units of wine. England therefore has the comparative advantage in cloth and Portugal in wine. Each specialises accordingly, and the terms of trade settle between the two domestic price ratios.
Gains from trade
With trade, each country consumes beyond its production possibility frontier. Gains arise from (i) exchange, as consumers get goods at the world price, and (ii) specialisation, as resources shift to the efficient sector. Under constant costs, specialisation is complete; under increasing opportunity costs it is partial.
Criticisms
The model ignores factor endowments, demand, economies of scale, and distributional effects. Empirically, MacDougall (1951) and Balassa (1963) found support: US/UK export ratios tracked relative labour productivity.
Conclusion
Despite its simplifying assumptions, comparative advantage remains the foundation of the case for free trade. Later theories (Heckscher-Ohlin, new trade theory) explain why costs differ but do not overturn the core insight that trade is positive-sum.